# The hourly wrapper writes this. It lives inside the repo, so without this rule every run
# would commit a log that changed only because the last run wrote to it - an endless commit
# loop that would bury the actual project history.
Sync-Workspace.log

# Obsidian, if this folder is ever opened as a vault.
.obsidian/

# Plugin runtime state. The healthcare Contracts Analyzer keeps a scratch SQLite database
# here; nothing under .claude/data/ is project content. Three reasons it must not be tracked:
#   1. The -wal is a write-ahead log and the -shm a shared-memory index. Both are meaningless
#      outside a live connection, so a commit of them stores a torn mid-transaction state.
#   2. They are rewritten on every run, so the hourly sync's `git add -A` re-commits them
#      forever - the same endless-loop problem as Sync-Workspace.log above.
#   3. SQLite memory-maps them, and Windows then refuses to let git unlink them. On
#      07-Sep-2026 this aborted a `git reset --hard` half-way and left the tree inconsistent
#      ("unable to unlink old '...data.sqlite-shm': Invalid argument"). A tracked file that a
#      running process can lock will break checkout, reset and merge without warning.
# Only .claude/data/ is ignored, so .claude/settings.json or project skills stay trackable.
.claude/data/
